## 3. The data (verified 2026-10-17 from the OpenML API)

- OpenML dataset 61, name `iris`, version 1, target `class`, licence "Public", uploaded 2014-04-06.
- ARFF: `https://openml.org/data/v1/download/61/iris.arff`, md5 `ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9` (matches OpenML's `md5_checksum`; I downloaded and checked it). Parquet: `https://data.openml.org/datasets/0000/0061/dataset_61.pq`.
- 150 instances, 4 numeric features (sepallength, sepalwidth, petallength, petalwidth; cm), 3 classes × 50 (balanced), no missing values.
- 3 exact duplicate rows (feature values and class). They can fall in train and test of the same fold; this is a property of the data, worth one sentence, not a reason to deduplicate (the objective says use the dataset).
- This is the UCI copy, which differs from Fisher's published data in two entries (rows 35 and 38 in UCI order; Bezdek et al. 1999). In this file rows 35 and 38 are both `4.9,3.1,1.5,0.1,Iris-setosa`. Worth a footnote or limitation; do not correct the data.
- Original source: Fisher (1936); data collected by Anderson.

## 4. Conventions and prior work the study should follow or compare against

Interval for repeated-CV accuracies (the analysis owner decides; the paper must name the method):
- The 50 fold accuracies are not independent (training sets overlap, and repetitions reuse the same 150 points). A naive t-interval over 50 folds is too narrow (Dietterich 1998; Nadeau & Bengio 2003; Bouckaert & Frank 2004).
- The usual convention for r×k repeated CV is the corrected resampled t (Nadeau & Bengio 2003; Bouckaert & Frank 2004): variance factor (1/(r·k) + n_test/n_train) = 1/50 + 1/4, with r·k − 1 = 49 df. This applies to each method's mean and to the paired difference.
- Simpler alternatives a reader would accept if labelled: t-interval over the 10 repetition means (df 9), which captures split variability only; or a bootstrap. Bates, Hastie & Tibshirani (2023) show CV intervals can undercover and propose nested-CV intervals; cite as limitation.
- With n = 150, one test fold has 30 points, so a single misclassification moves a fold accuracy by 1/30 ≈ 0.033; accuracies are discrete. The figure should show this granularity (per-fold or per-repetition points), not only means.

Model selection inside CV:
- Selecting k on the outer test data biases the estimate (Varma & Simon 2006), hence the objective's inner CV. Any preprocessing that learns from data (e.g. feature standardisation) must be fitted inside each training fold (a pipeline). Standardisation matters for kNN (Euclidean distance) and for regularised LR; the choice (with or without) must be stated and applied the same way to both methods.

Methods:
- k-NN: Cover & Hart (1967). Multinomial logistic regression: standard; cite the implementation (scikit-learn, Pedregosa et al. 2011, JMLR 12:2825–2830) with version.
- OpenML: Vanschoren et al. (2014).

Planning expectation (not a measurement; do not quote as a result): both methods are known to reach high accuracy on Iris (roughly in the mid-90s percent under CV), so the paired difference is expected to be small relative to its interval. The paper's claim should be whatever the interval shows, including "no detectable difference"; a null result is a valid finding here.

